Dedication ((optional))
Electronic conductive metal-organic frameworks (EC-MOFs) are a newly emerging type of porous conductive material.[1] Compared with traditional porous conductive materials, like porous carbons and porous metals, EC-MOFs possess crystalline state, size narrowly distributed and regularly arranged pore, tunable band gap, as well as designable charge transport pathway.[2] EC-MOFs have shown great potential application in field-effect transistor (FET), supercapacitor, thermoelectric device, oxygen reduction reaction electrocatalyst (ORR), chemiresistor gas sensor,etc.[1b,2a,2b,3] Up to now, the major EC-MOFs based devices are fabricated with powders or thick films[2b,3d 3e] Nevertheless, large grainsize and bad grain contact inhibit the fast transport of both electron and mass in above electrical devices. To improve further the performances of these devices, high quality EC-MOFs film with controllable thickness in nanometer and less than $100\ \mathsf{n m}$ is required.[3c, 4] Unfortunately, the fabrication of EC-MOF thin film with good control in nanometer scale over thickness, grain size, roughness and orientation, as far as we know, has not been achieved yet.
In this work, we report the controllable thin film fabrication of an EC-MOF, $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ , by a spray layer-by-layer (LbL) liquidphase epitaxial method for the first time. The thin film not only can be precisely prepared with thickness increment of \~2 nm in each growing cycle, but also shows smooth surface, good crystallinity and high orientation. These good qualities of the ECMOF thin film facilitate its application in high-performance semiconductor devices. As a proof of concept, high-performance chemiresistor gas sensors based on $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ thin films were fabricated and studied.
The crystal structure of $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ is shown in Figure 1a. In ab plane, Cu ions coordinate to HHTP ligands to form a twodimensional (2D) hexagonal layer. The hexagonal layers pack along the $c$ -axis in a slipped-parallel AB stacking model with an interval distance of $3.3\mathrm{~\AA~}$ 。resulting in a honeycomb-like porous structure. The one-dimensional channels have an open-window size of ${\sim}1.8$ nm and a large amount of polar organic functional groups on the wall. The strong charge delocalization between Cu ions and ligands endows this MOF with good electronic conductivity. To prepare $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ thin film, the substrates (e.g. sapphire, glass, $\mathsf{S i}/\mathsf{S i O}_{2}.$ ：quartz）were firstly treated with Piranha solution to obtain $-O H$ functionalized surface (Figure S1 Supporting Information (Sl)). After that, the functionalized substrates were alternatively exposed to the ethanolic solution of copper (ll) acetate $(0.1~\mathsf{m M})$ and the HHTP ligands $(0.01~\mathsf{m M})$ to epitaxially grow $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ thin film in a LbL fashion by a spray method (Figure 1b, details see Sl).[5] In this work, it is found that -OH groups can play the similar role of the organic selfassembly-monolayer to orient the MOF thin film growth. Between each spray steps, the substrate was rinsed with pure ethanol to remove unreacted reactants,which makes the thin
film growth controllable in thickness. With various growing cycles, the $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ thin films with different thickness were obtained, which were denoted as $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2^{-}}\mathsf{X C}$ (x is the growing cycles).
Figure 2a and 2b show the scan electron microscope (SEM) and atomic force microscopy (AFM) images of the typical top and cross-sectional view of the $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}\mathsf{-}40\mathsf{C}$ thin film. The observed thin film is dense and continuous. Similar results can be found for the thin films with different growing cycles (Figure S2). Due to its higher resolution at vertical dimension, AFM was employed to further confirm the thickness of the thin films with growing cycles less than 40 (Figure S3). Figure 2c shows the linear relationship between the growing cycle and the thickness of the thin film, which reveals the precisely controlled growth of $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2^{-}}\mathsf{X C}$ with average $2\mathsf{n m}$ increment in thickness for each growing cycle. AFM measurements further confirm the smooth and continuous surfaces for all $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}\mathsf{-X C}$ (Figure 2b and Figure S4). The calculated root mean square (RMS) surface roughness increases depending on the thickness of $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2^{-}}\mathsf{x C}$ ，but the highest value is less than $5\:\mathsf{n m}$ for the thin film with $100~\mathsf{n m}$ thickness (Figure 2d). The smooth growing process can be further confirmed by monitoring $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2^{-}}\mathsf{x C}$ on quartz substrate with UV-Vis spectrum. As shown in Figure 2e, the absorbance of the thin film increases with increasing the growing cycles. The intensity of the maximum absorption peak $(366~\mathsf{n m})$ is linearly proportional to the growing cycles, which is consistent with these observed bySEM and AFM measurements. Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) spectrum shows the pattern of the prepared $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}{\mathsf{-}}50\mathsf{C}$ matches well with that simulated from the crystal structure of $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ (Figure 2f), demonstrating the successful preparation of $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2^{-}}\mathsf{X C}$
The orientation nature of $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2^{-}}\mathsf{x C}$ was revealed by using transmission electron microscope (TEM) measurements. Selected area electron diffractions (SAED） of the thin film fragments peeled off from $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}\mathsf{-10C}$ and $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}.$ 50C match the hexagonal crystal structure of $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ 0 ${\sf\partial}a=b$ $=21.75\mathrm{~\AA~}$ $c=6.66\mathrm{~\AA~}$ [2a,3b] furtherconfirmingthecrtalline phase and purity of $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}{\mathsf{-x C}}$ (Figure 2g and h). Since the thickness  of $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}\mathsf{-}10\mathsf{C}$ is ${\sim}20\quad\mathsf{n m}$ and that of $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}..50\mathsf{C}$ is $\mathord{\sim}100\ \mathsf{n m}$ which are much smaller than the observed lateral sizes of the thin film fragments for TEM measurement $(\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}-10\mathsf{C}$ ? ${>}300$ nm， $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}.$ $500>5000~\mathsf{n m}_{.}$ ), the fragments should be peeled off parallel to the substrate. So the orientation of these fragments should be the same as that of $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2^{-}}\mathsf{x C}$ . The SAED patterns exhibit oriented-polycrystal-like dots arrays and the diameter of the electron beam used for SAED measurement is around $500~\mathsf{n m}$ which indicates the single crystal domain size in the thin film is up to hundreds nm. The rings of (hkO) can be clearly observed, while the rings of (0ol) are missing from the pattern. Similar results could be found on randomly selected other fragments (Figure S5). These results strongly imply the $c$ -axis orientation structure feature of $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}\mathsf{-x c}$ (Figure 2i). Determining the orientation by SAED method has been demonstrated feasible on textured $z_{\mathsf{n O}}$ seeds thin film[] and layered salts thin film.[7]
Ammonia $(N H_{3})$ is a toxic, flammable and explosive gas but utilized extensively in chemical industries, fertilizer factories, etc.8] It is also a typical biomarker for the detection of kidney and liver diseases[9] for breath analysis.[10] At present, realizing high sensitive and selective detection of $N H_{3}$ at room temperature (RT)is still a big challenge. In view of its high RT conductivity of $0.02\ \textup{S}\ \mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ (Figure S6), $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2^{-}}\mathsf{x C}$ were utilized to fabricate chemiresistor sensors for the $N H_{3}$ detection of high sensitivity and selectivity. $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2^{-}}\mathsf{X C}$ based chemiresistor sensors (Figure S1a) were tested in a home-made sensing system reported in our previous works (details see Sl).[11 Figure 3a shows the typical response-recovery curve of $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ 10C to $N H_{3}$ with different concentrations. Upon exposure to $N H_{3}$ the sensor resistance exhibited a pronounced increment, which is the typical behaviour of a p-type semiconductor. The current curve presents good response-recovery to a broad range of the $N H_{3}$ concentrations (1 to 100 ppm). Good repeatability of response to 100 ppm $N H_{3}$ with low coefficient of variation $(1.54\%)$ can be observed.The average resistance change of $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}\mathsf{-}10\mathsf{C}$ toward 100 ppm $N H_{3}$ was estimated to be $129\%$ . It is ${\sim}12$ times higher than that of the reported sensors based on $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ powders[3e] or nanorods[3], and ${\sim}5$ times higher than the $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ thick film gas sensor (Figure S7 and S8). Notably, this response value is among the highest ones of the reported RT sensors (Table S1, SI).
Figure 3b shows the log-log plots of response $(\mathsf{R}_{9\mathsf{a s}}/\mathsf{R}_{\mathsf{a i r}}-1)$ vs concentration of $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}\mathsf{-}10\mathsf{C}$ sensor toward $N H_{3}$ . The good linearity in the range of 1 to 10o ppm is in accordance with typical chemiresistor gas sensor.[11l A $\beta$ value of 0.472 is close to 0.5, which is generally indicative of a fully regular microstructure of the nanograins $(\beta>0.5,$ disordered microstructure; $\beta{<}O.5,$ local agglomeration or zones).[12] The theoretical limit of detection (LOD) can be calculated to be ${\sim}0.5$ ppm from the simulated
linear equation by setting response $\mathbf{\tau}=\mathbf{\tau}$ $10\%$ [13] Figure $\ensuremath{3\mathrm{c}}$ illustrates the response time (the time required increasing the resistance to $90\%$ of the saturation value) and recovery time (the time required decreasing the saturated resistance to its $10\%$ of $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}\mathsf{-}10\mathsf{C}$ when exposed to 100 ppm $N H_{3}$ and dry synthetic air, respectively. Fast response and recovery times with the values of 1.36 and 9.11 min can be estimated, respectively. Compared with $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ powders based sensor, the response and recovery of $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ -10C are speeded up by $54\%$ and $10\%$ ， respectively (Figure S7). Notably, the response speed of $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ -10C is among one of the fastest value for various reported $N H_{3}$ RT gas sensors (Table S1, SI), which might be further improved by introducing proper foreign metal ions or organic ligand that have relatively weaker interactions with NH3 into $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ and by further improving the quality of the thin film.
The cross-sensitivities of $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ -10C toward 100 ppm of typical reducing gases were shown in Figure 3d. The sensor showed responses lower than $20\%$ toward most of these gases. Even for RT high active gas molecules, such as acetone and ethanol, they can only induce responses between $30\%$ and $40\%$ The value of selectivity ( $\mathsf{S}=$ Response $(N H_{3})/$ Response (gas)) of $N H_{3}$ toward different reducing gas varied from 4.55 to 8.25, which is good enough for precise detection of $N H_{3}$ among these interfering gases.
The effect of the thickness to the sensor performances was systemically evaluated via conducting growing cycle dependent gas sensing measurements. As shown in Figure 3e and S9, faster response and recovery of thinner film can be clearly observed due to the easier contact of guest molecules with the active sites with minimal diffusion barriers in these films. For sensors with growing cycles of 30 or higher, their responserecovery speed were even lower than these of powder based sensor (Figure S7), because the powder one possesses a significant amount of macro/meso-pores due to loose accumulation of crystallites for relatively better gas diffusion. It is also observed that the thinner films have the higher responses to $N H_{3}$
$\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}\mathsf{-X C}$ sensors have excellent long-term stability and reproducibility. After $_3$ months, $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}\mathsf{-}10\mathsf{C}$ remains $88.4\%$ of its original response towards $N H_{3}$ (Figure S10). The log-log plots of response vs concentration collected from four sensors for each thickness (10, 30 and 50 growing cycles, Figure S11) have good linearity and narrow error bars during the sensing measurements of $N H_{3}$ with different concentrations (Figure 3f).
Although the exact mechanism for the sensing performances of $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2^{-}}\mathsf{X C}$ is still under revealing,we found that 1) the crystal structure of $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ remains unchanged before and after exposed to saturated NH3 gas, as observed from PXRD spectra (Figure S7b); 2) FT-lR spectra (Figure 4a) show typical peaks for $N H_{3}-C u$ at 3170,877 and $856~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ as well as the red shift of $v(C=0)$ and v(C-O)[14] These results  suggest strong interactions between $N H_{3}$ and the framework of $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ which might be the origin for the high selectivity of $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ to $N H_{3}$ . Similar results has also been reported by Dinca[3e] and Mirica's groups.[3fl Ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS) measurements show the Fermi level of $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ -10C increased by $1.13\mathsf{e V}$ after adsorption of $N H_{3}$ (Figure 4b). This is $\mathsf{a}$ typical phenomenon when a n-type doping happen to a p-type semiconductor.[15] $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ is a $\mathsf{p}$ -type sensor material and hole dominates its charge transport. The recombination between the hole in $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ and the doped electron from analytes decreases carrier concentration and increases the resistance of the sensor[3e,15,16]The $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2^{-}}\mathsf{x C}$ is very thin $(<100\ \mathsf{n m})$ ， which provides short gas diffusion length and high ratio of accessible surface area. Moreover, $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2^{-}}\mathsf{X C}$ has high crystallinity and large single crystal domain size, which can facilitate effective charge transport. All of these contribute to the high performances of our sensors.
In summary, the controlled preparation of the high quality thin film based on an electronically conductive MOF, $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ ，was reported for the first time. With a spray LbL assembly method, the thin films prepared not only possess good crystallinity and high orientation, but also large crystal domain size, dense packing， smooth surface and well controlled thickness. $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ thin film with the thickness of $20~\mathsf{n m}$ has excellent room termperature sensing performances: selective detection of $N H_{3}$ toward 10 typical interference gases; highest response with the average resistance change toward 100 ppm $N H_{3}$ as high as $129\%$ ; very short response time with the value of 1.36 min; excellent long-term stability and reproducibility with $88.4\%$ response retention after 3 months. These results indicate the potential application of RT MOF sensor for real-time monitoring and timely alarming of toxic, explosive and flammable $N H_{3}$ . Since MOF materials can be flexibly designed to obtain high affinity and unique selectivity to target gas, MOF based chemiresistor sensors offer a high possibility to detect a single gas at RT, providing an exciting and powerful platform for the development of new electrical devices.
